1
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:06,230
In 2003, there was no new Star Wars movie.

2
00:00:06,280 --> 00:00:08,690
And no new Harry Potter movie.

3
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:12,690
LEGO's sales fell by about a quarter.

4
00:00:12,740 --> 00:00:15,350
In the United States, by about a third.

5
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:17,970
More than half of the drop came from the movie sets.

6
00:00:18,020 --> 00:00:21,550
And stores that had stocked up the year before now ordered less.

7
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:24,650
That's when the hidden cost showed up.

8
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:29,370
Every one of those thousands of molds, bins and production runs still had to be paid for,

9
00:00:29,420 --> 00:00:31,230
whether a set sold or not.

10
00:00:32,280 --> 00:00:40,970
In January 2004, LEGO announced a record loss for 2003: about one point four billion kroner before tax.

11
00:00:41,020 --> 00:00:42,755
The turnaround manager left.

12
00:00:42,805 --> 00:00:47,670
Kjeld Kirk Kristiansen took over the day-to-day running of the company himself.

13
00:00:48,720 --> 00:00:54,230
When the finance team went through the company product by product, they found something worse.

14
00:00:54,280 --> 00:00:59,310
About sixty percent of sales were producing all of the company's operating profit.

15
00:00:59,360 --> 00:01:02,670
Everything else, together, was losing money.

16
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:07,670
One finance executive later said LEGO had been destroying value since 1993.

17
00:01:08,820 --> 00:01:12,170
And 2004 was turning out even worse.

18
00:01:12,220 --> 00:01:17,390
For the first time, it was a real question whether LEGO, as an independent family company,

19
00:01:17,440 --> 00:01:18,430
would survive.
